10.3  Time zones, UTC and daylight saving

TL;DR
  - The kernel counts time in UTC. Time zones are applied when time is
    displayed, by the C library, from the rules in /etc/localtime.
  - Each process can use another zone: TZ=Asia/Tokyo date.
  - Store and compute in UTC; convert to local time only to show it.

      timedatectl set-timezone Europe/Paris
      TZ=America/New_York date
      date -u                          # UTC

On your machine
  Zone      Europe/Paris
  RTC       the hardware clock keeps UTC
  Source    tsc (also available: hpet, acpi_pm)
  Sync      systemd-timesyncd, synchronised
  Booted    2026-10-11 06:26 UTC

Pitfalls
  - When clocks go back, a local time like 02:30 happens twice; when they go
    forward, it doesn't happen at all. Jobs scheduled then may run twice or be
    skipped: schedule them outside 01:00–03:00, or in UTC.
  - A day isn't always 24 hours, in local time: add days with a date library,
    not + 86400.
  - Zone rules change by law, sometimes at short notice: keep tzdata updated,
    and use names like Europe/Paris, not offsets like +01:00.
  - Dual boot with Windows: Windows keeps the hardware clock in local time,
    Linux in UTC, so one of them shows the wrong hour.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 10.3 --why

Related
  Commands  timedatectl, date
  Paths     /etc/localtime, /usr/share/zoneinfo/
  See also  idk 10.4 Timestamps: Unix time, 2038 and leap seconds
  See also  idk 10.1 Wall clock and monotonic clock: measuring time right
